I love what the Cyborg wrote, but I don't think it answers the question asked. I see the argument as one of the importance of the idea that humans have an experiment, but not as a specific model, in this case, the cyborg model. I think the Cyborg is correct in saying that this question is a specific model of human development. However, he does not answer the question, and the argument does not really show how to apply the argument to human development. I think a lot of the argument is also a bit circular. For example, he says that the cyborg model is very important because it "does not forget to be the object." It doesn't seem like this is true, because humans are not objects, they are subjects, and as such, their identity is the result of the actions they take. This is probably true, but it doesn't seem to make the argument any stronger, and in fact, it seems to contradict itself.
