As you see it in the argument above, the focus is not on the concept of experiment, but on the idea of cyborg model. It could be that Loom is a good example of the development of a cyborg model, but that's an aside from the original question. I think the point of the question is about the idea of experiment and how it relates to the model of cyborg. This is where my answer is coming from. In the argument above, I have argued that an experiment is a model of human development. I have argued that one of the strengths of the cyborg model is that humans don't just live in the world, they are part of the world. But this model is also problematic. First, it's a model for individual subjects, not for groups of subjects. Second, it's an individual model, not a collective model. To put it simply, the cyborg is an individual whose identity is defined by the actions they take, and thus there is no shared identity, no collective. This is a problem because the collective is important. This is where the cyborg model fails in the argument above because it's not really a model for the development of the collective, it's a model for the development of the individual. I think the loom model is a step in the direction of developing a model for the collective because it is an ensemble of individuals who live together and who, as such, are part of a collective. The way to put it is that loom is a collective model.
